[{"category":"section","location":"#Paramorph","page":"Home","text":"Paramorph.jl describes parameter geometry with a deliberately tiny interface. A parameter space connects a flat unconstrained vector, suitable for optimization, to the natural constrained Julia values used by a model.\n\nThere is one open hook and five operations:\n\np = param_space(x)\n\ndimension(p)\nnames(p)\nexample(p)\nη = constrain(p, θ)\nθ = unconstrain(p, η)\n\nThat is the complete conceptual API. Constructors such as Pos, Simplex, SPD, and Correlation are only vocabulary for describing different parameter geometries.","title":"Paramorph"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-complete-interface","page":"Home","text":"","title":"The complete interface"},{"category":"section","location":"#Representation-model","page":"Home","text":"The package deliberately keeps only two mathematical representations:\n\nflat unconstrained vector  <---->  natural constrained value\n                          constrain\n                        unconstrain\n\nThe unconstrained side is always a vector. The constrained side can be a scalar, vector, matrix, or tuple depending on the parameter space. names(p) is metadata attached to the logical constrained parameters; it does not force the constrained value into a NamedTuple.\n\nFor example:\n\np = (\n    RealVec(:μ, 3),\n    Pos(:σ),\n    Correlation(:R, 3),\n)\n\nθ = zeros(dimension(p))\nη = constrain(p, θ)\n\nnames(p)\n# (:μ, :σ, :R)\n\ndimension(p)\n# 7\n\nunconstrain(p, η) ≈ θ\n# true","title":"Representation model"},{"category":"section","location":"#Space-constructors","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Space constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"#Scalar-spaces","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Scalar spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-scalar-parameters","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Related scalar parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"#Vector,-matrix,-and-structured-spaces","page":"Home","text":"SPD(:Σ, n) describes an arbitrary symmetric positive-definite matrix. Correlation(:R, n) describes a positive-definite correlation matrix with unit diagonal. Both return full matrices on the constrained side.","title":"Vector, matrix, and structured spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"#Naming-wrappers","page":"Home","text":"Tuples of spaces form Cartesian products. Prefixed changes logical names but not the underlying transformation.","title":"Naming wrappers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Distributions.jl-extension","page":"Home","text":"The bundled extension associates common Distributions.jl models with parameter spaces through the same param_space hook used by downstream packages. The core package itself has no dependency on Distributions.jl.\n\nusing Paramorph\nusing Distributions\n\nX = MvNormal(zeros(3), [1.0 0.2 0.1; 0.2 1.0 0.3; 0.1 0.3 1.0])\np = param_space(X)\n\n(names(p), dimension(p))\n((:μ, :Σ), 9)\n\nThe constrained values retain their constructor-level shapes:\n\nμ, Σ = example(p)\n(size(μ), size(Σ))\n((3,), (3, 3))\n\nFor MvNormalCanon, the matrix J is a precision matrix. Geometrically it is an arbitrary symmetric positive-definite matrix, so its parameter space is SPD(:J, n).","title":"Distributions.jl extension"},{"category":"section","location":"#Automatic-differentiation","page":"Home","text":"There is no differentiation-specific extension and no Jacobian API. constrain is implemented with ordinary generic Julia operations, so AD packages can pass through it directly.\n\nusing ForwardDiff, Paramorph\n\np = (Id(:μ), Pos(:σ))\n\nForwardDiff.gradient([0.3, -0.2]) do θ\n    μ, σ = constrain(p, θ)\n    μ^2 + σ\nend\n\n","title":"Automatic differentiation"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.param_space","page":"Home","text":"param_space(object)\n\nReturn the parameter-space description associated with object.\n\nparam_space is an open generic function. Paramorph.jl provides the space constructors and transformations, while downstream packages define methods for their own objects.\n\nparam_space(x) associates an object or type with its parameter-space description. It is an open generic function and is normally the only function a downstream package extends.\n\nimport Paramorph: param_space\n\nstruct MyModel\n    n::Int\nend\n\nparam_space(m::MyModel) = (\n    Id(:μ),\n    Pos(:σ),\n    Simplex(:weights, m.n),\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.param_space"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.dimension","page":"Home","text":"dimension(space)\n\nReturn the dimension of the underlying unconstrained space, that is the dimension of the vector of unconstrained parameters. \n\ndimension(p) is the number of scalar coordinates in the unconstrained representation. Equivalently, constrain(p, θ) expects length(θ) == dimension(p).\n\nA structured constrained parameter does not change this rule. For example, a 3 × 3 correlation matrix has three free coordinates:\n\np = Correlation(:R, 3)\ndimension(p) == 3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.dimension"},{"category":"function","location":"#Base.names","page":"Home","text":"names(space)\n\nReturn the list of symbols corresponding to the names of the constrained parameters.\n\nnames(p) returns the names of the logical constrained parameters. A vector or matrix parameter has one name, not one name per scalar entry.\n\np = (RealVec(:μ, 3), SPD(:Σ, 3))\nnames(p)\n# (:μ, :Σ)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.names"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.example","page":"Home","text":"example(space)\n\nReturn an example of a constraint parameter from the space. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.example"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.constrain","page":"Home","text":"constrain(space, par)\n\nReturn the constraint version of the parameter par according to the space.\n\nexample(p) returns one canonical value in the constrained representation. It is defined from the origin of the unconstrained chart:\n\nexample(p) == constrain(p, zeros(dimension(p)))\n\nIt is intended as a convenient valid representative of the space, not as a statistical default or fitted value.\n\nconstrain(p, θ) maps a flat unconstrained vector to its natural constrained representation.\n\nThe output keeps the model-level Julia structure:\n\nconstrain(Pos(:σ), [0.0])\n# 1.0\n\nconstrain(RealVec(:μ, 3), zeros(3))\n# [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\nconstrain(SPD(:Σ, 2), zeros(3))\n# [1.0 0.0; 0.0 1.0]\n\nFor a Cartesian product of spaces, the constrained result is a tuple of the logical parameter values:\n\np = (RealVec(:μ, 3), Pos(:σ), Correlation(:R, 3))\nη = constrain(p, zeros(dimension(p)))\n# (μ_vector, σ_scalar, R_matrix)\n\nThe constrained side is therefore not flattened merely for the convenience of an optimizer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.constrain"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.unconstrain","page":"Home","text":"unconstrain(space, par)\n\nReturn the unconstraint version of the parameter par according to the space.\n\nunconstrain(p, η) performs the inverse transformation: it accepts the natural constrained value and returns the flat unconstrained vector.\n\nFor interior points of a chart,\n\nθ = randn(dimension(p))\nunconstrain(p, constrain(p, θ)) ≈ θ\n\nClosed boundaries may naturally correspond to infinite unconstrained coordinates; this is part of the geometry of the chosen space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.unconstrain"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.Id","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter named name with values in ℝ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Id"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.Pos","page":"Home","text":"A strictly positive scalar parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Pos"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.NonNeg","page":"Home","text":"A nonnegative scalar parameter; zero maps back to -Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.NonNeg"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.Neg","page":"Home","text":"A strictly negative scalar parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Neg"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.Prob","page":"Home","text":"A probability parameter in [0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Prob"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.ProbOpen","page":"Home","text":"A probability parameter in (0, 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.ProbOpen"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.ProbOpenLeft","page":"Home","text":"A probability parameter in (0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.ProbOpenLeft"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.ProbOpenRight","page":"Home","text":"A probability parameter in [0, 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.ProbOpenRight"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.Lower","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter strictly greater than lower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Lower"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.LowerClosed","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter greater than or equal to lower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.LowerClosed"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.Bounded","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter in [lower, upper].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Bounded"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.BoundedOpen","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter in (lower, upper).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.BoundedOpen"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.BoundedOpenLeft","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter in (lower, upper].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.BoundedOpenLeft"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.BoundedOpenRight","page":"Home","text":"A scalar parameter in [lower, upper).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.BoundedOpenRight"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.Ordered","page":"Home","text":"Two real scalar parameters satisfying first < second.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Ordered"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.PosOrdered","page":"Home","text":"Two positive scalar parameters satisfying 0 < first < second.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.PosOrdered"},{"category":"type","location":"#Paramorph.Between","page":"Home","text":"Three scalar parameters satisfying lower ≤ value ≤ upper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Between"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.RealVec","page":"Home","text":"An unconstrained real vector parameter with n entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.RealVec"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.PosVec","page":"Home","text":"An n-component vector parameter with strictly positive entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.PosVec"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.ProbVec","page":"Home","text":"An n-component vector parameter with entries in [0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.ProbVec"},{"category":"function","location":"#Paramorph.RealMat","page":"Home","text":"An unconstrained real m × n matrix parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.RealMat"},{"category":"type","location":"#Paramorph.Simplex","page":"Home","text":"A probability vector of length n constrained to the simplex.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Simplex"},{"category":"type","location":"#Paramorph.SPD","page":"Home","text":"An n × n symmetric positive-definite matrix parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.SPD"},{"category":"type","location":"#Paramorph.Correlation","page":"Home","text":"An n × n positive-definite correlation-matrix parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Correlation"},{"category":"type","location":"#Paramorph.Prefixed","page":"Home","text":"Prefix the logical parameter names of space without changing its values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Paramorph.Prefixed"}]
